1	USE CASE OVERVIEW
Issue:
Incident- and request-related email notifications generate excessive, low-value email traffic, resulting in user fatigue, reduced visibility of meaningful updates, and increased reliance on the service desk to interpret status or required actions.

Several notifications have been identified as “heavy hitters” that contribute little actionable value, while others would be better suited to consolidated, digest-style communications rather than immediate individual emails.
1	 USE CASES
End users and IT users receive multiple emails for routine task and request updates (assignments, work notes, opened-on-behalf activity, etc.). Due to volume and inconsistent content clarity, important updates are often overlooked, misunderstood, or require follow-up calls to the service desk.

The business wants to reduce email volume while improving the quality and clarity of communications, without negatively impacting operational workflows or user expectations.
1.1	USE CASE 1 – INCIDENT EMAIL NOTIFICATION OPTIMIZATION
Objective	Reduce overall notification email volume by removing low-value notifications.
Consolidate selected high-volume notifications into digest-based summaries.
Improve clarity and usefulness of email communications.
Leverage ServiceNow native email digest capability where possible.
Clearly document technical constraints and tradeoffs (e.g., reply-to-record behavior).

Actors	ServiceNow Engineer, ServiceNow BA (Brandon Hanshew), Trevor Miller, Cara Cormier
Requirements	Notification Removal:
- Incident Closed
- Catalog task worknoted (to assignee)
- Incident worknoted for ITIL
- RITM Open RequestedFor Notification

Digest-Enabled Notifications:
- Email assigned to group (sc_task)
- Email assigned to (sc_task)
- Request opened on behalf of

Digest Content Requirements:
Each digest item must include:
- Record number
- Short description
- Relevant assignment or request context
- Direct link to the record

User Preference Behavior:
- Digest delivery respects standard ServiceNow notification preferences.
- Users without digest enabled continue receiving individual notifications.

Reply-to-Record:
- Ensure that any email response to the generated emails can be associated to the work notes of the appropriate record in the platform (note: this may not be possible with attempting to use a single email for digest of ALL incidents - that's OK. Stay within confines of what we can do).
- If reply-to-record is not feasible for digest emails, users must be directed to update records in-platform.

Acceptance Criteria	•	Identified notifications no longer send emails in production.
•	Digest-enabled notifications expose a digest option in user preferences.
•	Users who enable digest receive consolidated emails.
•	Digest emails include required context and working links.
•	Users who do not enable digest experience no behavior change.
•	Platform limitations are documented and approved.
Notes	Reference link: https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/yokohama/platform-administration/configure-email-digest.html

Dev Notes/Implementation	Notification Deactivation:
•	Disable notifications via Active flag or conditional logic.
•	Confirm notifications are not reused elsewhere.
Digest Configuration:
•	Enable Allow Digest on selected notifications.
•	Configure Digest Subject, Digest HTML, and Digest Separator.
•	Optionally reuse an email template.
Digest Frequency:
•	Confirm acceptable cadence (daily/weekly).
•	Native scheduling preferred unless custom cadence is required.
Reply-to-Record Constraint:
•	Multi-record digests cannot reliably support reply-to-record.
•	Provide clear guidance and links directing users to update records directly in ServiceNow.
Digest Configuration (Native)

ServiceNow supports digest delivery directly on notification records.

Implementation steps:
•	Navigate to System Notification > Email > Notifications
•	Open each digest-enabled notification
•	Enable Allow Digest
•	Configure the What Digest will contain tab:
o	Digest Subject – meaningful, timeframe-aware subject
o	Digest HTML – structured content with record variables
o	Digest Separator (HTML) – clear visual separation between items
o	Optional: reuse an email template for header/footer consistency

Important behavior notes:
•	Digest emails are generated only for users who:
o	Are valid recipients per notification rules, and
o	Have enabled digest in notification preferences
•	If a user does not meet both conditions, individual notifications will still send.


